.TH GZIP 1
.SH NAME
gzip, gunzip \- compression and decompression utilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gzip
[
.B \-ckv
] [
.BI \- level
] [
.IR file ...
]
.br
.B gunzip
[
.B \-ck
] [
.IR file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Gzip
and
.B gunzip
perform data compression and decompression
compatible with the Unix
.I gzip
file format,
which uses a hybrid Lempel-Ziv 1977 and Huffman compression algorithm
called `deflate'.
If no arguments are given
.B gzip
compresses each
.I file
replacing it by a file of the same
name with the
.B .gz
suffix appended.
If no files are given, standard input
and standard output are used.
.I Level
is a single decimal digit between 1 and 9;
higher numbers give better compression.
The default
.I level
is 6.
The
.B \-v
option causes
.B gzip
to report the compression ratio of each file that
it compresses.
The
.B \-c
option writes the compressed data to standard output and
leaves the input file unchanged.
The
.B \-k
option keeps (does not delete) the input file.
.PP
.B Gunzip
performs the opposite operation to
.BR gzip ;
each
.I file
(which must have a
.B .gz
suffix) is uncompressed, and replaced
by a file of the same name with the
.B .gz
suffix removed. If no files are given,
standard input and standard output are used.
The
.B \-c
option writes the decompressed data to standard output and
leaves the input file unchanged.
The
.B \-k
option keeps (does not delete) the input
.B .gz
file.
.SH EXAMPLES
The
.B \-c
flag makes it easy to write the customary
.I zcat
helper as a one-line shell function:
.PP
.EX
fn zcat { gunzip -c $* }
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gzip.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/gunzip.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bzip2 (1),
.IR filter (2),
.IR filter-deflate (2)
